Confirmed. On pristine the test passes; on the mutant build it fails with `1024 !== 1`.

The change drops the `^` anchor from `parseRegExp`, so the pattern can now match anywhere in the string rather than only from the start. Previously invalid inputs now parse with the unit multiplier instead of falling through to the plain `parseInt` bytes fallback:

- `' 1kb'` → pristine `1` (regex fails, `parseInt` → 1 byte), mutant `1024`
- `'foo1kb'` → pristine `null`, mutant `1024`
- `'1e2kb'` → pristine `1`, mutant `2048`

This is observable by any caller of `bytes.parse`, so the change is a genuine behavioural defect.

